 ## Operating characteristics
 
-*   **Time Complexity:** Initialization time is dominated by the SfM process, which is $O(N^2)$ or $O(N \log N)$ depending on the implementation, where $N$ is the number of images/features. For COLMAP, this can take several minutes for large scenes [S5, unofficial]. Random initialization is significantly faster (seconds) but yields lower quality [S5, S7, unofficial].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** The initial memory footprint is proportional to the number of SfM points $N$. Each point requires storage for position (3 floats), color (3 floats), scale (3 floats), rotation (4 floats), and opacity (1 float), totaling approximately 14-16 bytes per point, plus SH coefficients if used.
-*   **Performance:** SfM initialization typically yields higher PSNR (e.g., 26.05 vs 20.42 on Average-30K) and better background quality compared to random initialization [S7, unofficial]. However, it is slower to initialize [S5, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Provides geometrically accurate starting points, improving convergence and final reconstruction quality. Avoids the "cloud-like" artifacts associated with random initialization in sparse regions.
-*   **Limitations:** Requires high-quality SfM reconstruction. Fails or performs poorly if the input images have insufficient overlap or texture. Dense MVS initialization is discouraged due to bias and computational waste [S8, unofficial].
+*   **Performance:** SfM initialization typically yields higher PSNR (e.g., 26.05 vs 20.42 on Average-30K) compared to random initialization [S7, unofficial]. COLMAP initialization takes ~6.0 minutes, while random initialization takes ~0.1 seconds [S5, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Provides geometrically accurate starting points, improving convergence and final reconstruction quality [S7, S8, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** Requires high-quality SfM reconstruction. Dense MVS initialization is discouraged due to bias and computational waste [S8, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
 
-The method originates from the seminal 3D Gaussian Splatting paper [1], which introduced the use of 3D Gaussians for explicit radiance field representation. The initialization from sparse SfM points was chosen to provide a geometric prior that guides the optimization, as opposed to random initialization which relies entirely on the optimizer to discover the scene geometry. COLMAP is the standard tool used for this purpose [S2, S8, unofficial]. Subsequent work has explored variants such as random initialization for synthetic data [S2, unofficial], MVS-guided initialization [S12], and neural prediction of geometry [S2, unofficial].
+The initialization from sparse SfM points was chosen to provide a geometric prior that guides the optimization, as opposed to random initialization which relies entirely on the optimizer to discover the scene geometry [S2, unofficial]. COLMAP is the standard tool used for this purpose [S2, S8, unofficial]. Subsequent work has explored variants such as random initialization for synthetic data [S2, unofficial], MVS-guided initialization [S13], and neural prediction of geometry [S2, unofficial].
 
